(2,'Fe³⁺: it oxidizes I⁻ but not Cl⁻ under standard conditions.','Fe³⁺/Fe²⁺ at+0.77 V lies between I₂/I⁻ at+0.54 and Cl₂/Cl⁻ at+1.36.','2Fe³⁺+2I⁻→2Fe²⁺+I₂ has E°=+0.23 V; the corresponding chloride oxidation would give−0.59 V.'),
(3,'(a) Yes:3Mg+2Al³⁺→3Mg²⁺+2Al. (b) No spontaneous standard reaction for Cl⁻+I₂. (c) Yes in the direct table model:Hg²⁺+2Ag→Hg+2Ag⁺.','The first potential is−1.66−(−2.37)=+0.71 V; the second is0.54−1.36=−0.82 V.','For mercury/silver,0.85−0.80=+0.05 V. Actual passivation and possible mercury(I) intermediates can complicate the ideal table predictions.'),
(4,'Metals: generally bottom right, give electrons, transfer electrons to oxidants above them on the left. Non-metals: stronger oxidants toward top left, take electrons, accept electrons from reductants below them on the right.','Left-hand species are oxidants and right-hand species are their reduced forms.','A stronger left oxidant paired with a lower-potential right reductant gives a positive E° difference.'),
(5,'(a) Fe+Sn²⁺→Fe²⁺+Sn, spontaneous. (b) F₂+2Br⁻→2F⁻+Br₂, spontaneous. (c) No spontaneous standard redox reaction for Cu+NaI(aq).','For(a),E°=−0.14−(−0.45)=+0.31 V; for(b),2.87−1.09=+1.78 V.','Na⁺ is too weak an oxidant to oxidize copper, while I⁻ itself is a reductant; nitrate and potassium are spectators.'),
(6,'(a) 2Fe³⁺+2I⁻→2Fe²⁺+I₂. (b) Br₂+2Fe²⁺→2Br⁻+2Fe³⁺.','For(a),Fe³⁺ at0.77 V oxidizes I⁻ whose pair is0.54 V.','For(b),Br₂ at1.09 V oxidizes Fe²⁺ whose pair is0.77 V. Each balanced reaction transfers two electrons.'),
