(8,'No. FeCl₃ can corrode Al:Al+3Fe³⁺→Al³⁺+3Fe²⁺.','Fe³⁺/Fe²⁺ is+0.77 V and Al³⁺/Al is−1.66 V.','E°cell=+2.43 V strongly favours Al oxidation; an oxide film can delay but not remove the thermodynamic tendency, and chloride can undermine passivation.'),
(9,'(a) Reverse is spontaneous. (b) Forward is spontaneous.','For(a),E°forward=0.15−0.77=−0.62 V.','For(b),acidic dichromate reduction coupled to Cu oxidation gives1.23−0.34=+0.89 V.'),
(10,'Mg+2H⁺→Mg²⁺+H₂.','Magnesium oxidation is Mg→Mg²⁺+2e⁻.','Hydrogen reduction is2H⁺+2e⁻→H₂; their sum has E°cell=0−(−2.37)=+2.37 V.'),
(11,'Acidic nitrate oxidizes silver; H⁺ alone does not. Representative net equation:3Ag+NO₃⁻+4H⁺→3Ag⁺+NO+2H₂O.','Nitrate/NO at+0.96 V exceeds Ag⁺/Ag at+0.80 V.','H⁺/H₂ at0 V gives a negative cell potential for silver oxidation, explaining the difference from nonoxidizing HCl.'),
(12,'2Na+2H₂O→2Na⁺+2OH⁻+H₂. OH⁻ makes phenolphthalein pink; released heat can ignite the hydrogen.','Two Na atoms each lose one electron.','2H₂O+2e⁻→H₂+2OH⁻ consumes those electrons and produces both the alkaline pink tracks and hydrogen.'),
