(15,'Electrically connect Zn or Mg sacrificial anodes to steel in the conductive marine environment;the attached metal oxidizes and supplies electrons,keeping steel cathodic.','Choose a metal whose reduction potential is more negative than iron.','Its oxidation supports reduction on the steel and suppresses Fe dissolution. Maintain contact and replace the consumed sacrificial anodes.'),
(16,'Zinc provides a barrier and sacrificial protection:at scratches,connected Zn oxidizes preferentially and keeps exposed iron cathodic.','Zn²⁺/Zn at−0.76 V is below Fe²⁺/Fe at−0.45 V.','Zn→Zn²⁺+2e⁻ supplies electrons until zinc is depleted;unlike a less reactive coating,it can protect even where the barrier is broken.'),
(17,'The oxidant and reductant need not collide directly;electron transfer occurs at separate electrode interfaces connected by a wire and ionic circuit.','The reducing agent gives electrons to an anode.','Those electrons reach the cathode and are accepted by an oxidant. Local interfacial contact and activation barriers still matter,even though the two bulk reactants are separated.')
